AB  - For transactions, up to date blockchain protocols primarily depend on digital signature schemes like RSA and ECC, that are built on traditional cryptography. These cryptography algorithms are at risk by quantum algorithms such as Shor's and Grover's, that form part of the latest advances in quantum computing. To mitigate this risk of vulnerability, post-quantum cryptography (PQC) techniques that are safe from both classical and quantum attacks must be used. As a means to protect and verify transactions in the future, we show and evaluate post-quantum digital signature schemes in blockchain protocols. We outline the cryptographic base of post-quantum digital signatures, consider their suitability for blockchain networks, and present practical deployment implications according to recent National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) standardizations. Our methodology shows that using PQC not only reduces the threat of quantum adversaries but also enables the development of secure, decentralized financial and information exchange systems for the future quantum era.  Β© 2026 IEEE.
