AB  - Smart home technology is becoming increasingly popular, but it is not without security concerns. Such concerns are unauthorized access, theft of data, malware attacks, and privacy concerns. Most security systems are vulnerable since they rely on a single point of control, hence easy to target for cyberattacks. Blockchain can solve this using cryptographic methods, consensus rules, and smart contracts to make systems secure. It can improve the manner in which we verify identities, grant access, and protect data in smart homes. This chapter analyzes how blockchain can improve security in smart homes. It defines blockchainβ€™s fundamental principles and security features, and how it can be integrated with new technologies like the Internet of Things, edge computing, and artificial intelligence (AI). A number of security models based on blockchain, including decentralized identity management, intrusion detection systems, and privacy-preserving frameworks, are explained to show their advantages over other conventional security measures. In addition this paper conducts a comparative performance analysis of blockchainβ€™s security advantages, scalability, computational overhead, and energy consumption. Although there are benefits to blockchain technologies in smart homes (e.g., the benefits of decentralization), many barriers remain with significant challenges like time delays during transactions, the computational costs associated with transactions, and regulatory contexts. Future research directions should provide optimized blockchain protocols, integrate AI enhancements to security mechanisms, or design privacy-preserving frameworks. In addition it will be important to improve the abilities in 6G networks, federated learning, and quantum-resistant cryptography to support data points needed to provide real-time security for smart homes. This work concludes that blockchain has the possibility of changing the smart home with a trustless, resilient framework allowing for the elimination of central points of strength and/or vulnerabilities. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
