AB  - The mix of blockchain technology and digital twin technology opens up a completely new way to make healthcare interactions safer, more reliable, and more open. Digital twins allow for real-time replication of patient states, medical processes, and predictive models, but putting them into practice comes with a lot of privacy, trust, and cyber protection problems. To put your minds at ease, this study talks about Blockchain-Backed Secure Communication 3.0 for trustworthy digital twin healthcare systems. To keep private medical data safe and make sure that different healthcare systems can talk to each other, the suggested design combines an open ledger with smart contracts, lightweight consensus mechanisms, and advanced encryption methods. Threat models and attack surface analysis help make a safe communication system that includes encryption, key management, and finding strange behaviour using AI/ML techniques built into blockchain layers. Validation and proof of data origin improve system security by letting healthcare partners track and check information in real time. The system's usefulness can be seen in situations like responding to emergencies, keeping an eye on patients from afar, and using AI to help doctors make decisions. The framework thinks about problems like scale, energy efficiency, and legal compliance, such as HIPAA and GDPR. It also considers the possible addition of quantum-safe security methods. Results from experiments and design analysis show that security, trust, and communication all get better. As medical systems become more connected, Blockchain-Backed Secure Communication 3.0 makes them more reliable, more open, and safer for patients. It also builds a strong foundation for the next generation of digital twin healthcare environments. Β© 2026
