AB  - Smart home appliances are more susceptible to security threats with multiple cybersecurity vulnerabilities and thus end up leaking data, and failing to work. Existing forensic techniques fall short, and novel adaptive solutions are needed. This study introduces a novel Quantum-Driven Intrusion Detection and Anomaly Resolution (QIDAR) approach, which implements quantum computing fundamentals coupled with deep-learning hybrid analytics and blockchain-proof verification to discover intrusions of smart appliances and system failures. Further, in QIDAR, Quantum-Enhanced Cryptographic Logging (QECL) operates as QIDARβ€™s tamper-proof logging system through quantum cryptography to monitor anomalies extensively. Four fundamental processes from QIDAR enable the investigation of cyber incidents in smart appliances. Quantum-Assisted Anomaly Detection (Q-AAD) is based on integrating quantum operations into neural networks, which dynamically classify security risks. Adaptive Blockchain Ledger Forensics (ABLF) provides a secure method for storing forensic records using the blockchain as well as validating records through smart contracts. Multi-Modal AI Forensic Reconstruction (MMAFR) uses deep learning to rebuild cyberattacks from the past, allowing teams to predict threats and discover hidden data. Blockchain-Integrated Quantum Provenance Chain (BIQPC) is a quantum-secured forensic ledger that ensures traceability and non-repudiation of anomalies. QIDAR achieves exceptional achievements in consumer electronics, including 72% shorter forensic investigation duration as well as 98.5% efficient anomaly detection alongside a 67% superior data log tamper-resistance rate than traditional forensic methods. Β© 1975-2011 IEEE.
