AB  - Quantum computing as an inevitable technologyΒ can revolutionize many aspects of our society. One potential impactΒ is on cryptocurrency such as Bitcoin, which relies on proof-of-work mining to secure the underlying blockchain protocol. Miners empowered by quantum computers will have superior computational power to win the competition. The quantum advantage jeopardizesΒ the security and trustworthy of cryptocurrency and the transaction validation process by taking over a majority of the networkβ€™s computing power, known as a 51% attack. Fraudulent Bitcoin transactions in the form of double spending can happen, andΒ the emerging quantum miner could enable double spending and benefitΒ from it. How much double spending is optimal without causing tooΒ much β€inflationβ€? What shall be the optimal strategy of theΒ first quantum miner facing the competition from other quantum miners?Β What are the implications of having one or multiple quantum miners toΒ the security of the Bitcoin network? We conduct a novel game theoretic and economic analysis to address these questions. Simulation illustrates that quantum miners would have to collude to gainΒ from double spending in a quantum competitive environment.Β The distribution of cryptocurrency between quantum miners and classical miners and how cost-effective classical miners are can affectΒ the profitability and the sustainability of double spending as wellΒ as the collusion of quantum miners. Intensified quantum competition will decrease the chance of collusion and eventually makeΒ the Bitcoin network secure again. The critical point of quantum popularity that will eliminate double spending is found. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.
