AB  - Move, a novel blockchain programming language originally developed by Facebook's Libra (now Diem) project, is designed with a strong emphasis on security, flexibility, and performance. Its type-safe and resource-oriented programming model ensures that digital assets, such as tokens or smart contracts, cannot be accidentally duplicated or deleted - significantly enhancing the security of blockchain applications. While type safety plays a crucial role at the language level, the increasing capabilities of quantum computing have significantly undermined the security of cryptographic schemes based on prime factorization. As a result, such schemes are becoming inadequate in protecting user keys and signatures against quantum attacks. Post-quantum cryptographic algorithms enhance the Move language's resilience against quantum attacks; however, their increased computational complexity often leads to performance degradation. Despite this overhead, authentication within the Move ecosystem remains crucial even on resource-constrained embedded systems. This is especially relevant in decentralized environments where lightweight nodes - such as mobile wallets or IoT devices - must independently verify transactions without relying on trusted intermediaries. In this work, we integrate post-quantum cryptography into the Move infrastructure and further leverage hardware acceleration techniques on both server-class systems (x86 with AVX2) and embedded platforms (AArch64 with NEON and RISC-V with the V extension). We conduct experiments across these platforms and observe speedups ranging from 2.4 Γ— to 3.06 Γ— on server-class systems. For embedded platforms, we observe speedups ranging from 7.35 Γ— to 11.15 Γ— on AArch64, and from 3.86 Γ— to 4.47 Γ— on RISC-V.  Β© 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
