UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105017956488?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of Michigan - Flint, Department of Computer Science, Flint, MI, United States
AB  - Blockchain technology is widely applied across domains, from its well-known use in cryptocurrencies to emerging sectors like healthcare, real estate, and supply chain management. While current blockchains are considered secure due to their cryptographic foundations and consensus mechanisms, the advent of quantum computing poses a significant threat. A sufficiently powerful quantum computer could exploit Shor's algorithm to break widely used cryptographic schemes, such as RSA and ECDSA, jeopardizing blockchain security. Although quantum computers have not yet reached this level of capability, the potential development of such machines within the next decade makes it imperative to address this vulnerability. Signature schemes commonly used in blockchains, including ECDSA, EdDSA, and aggregate schemes like BLS in proof-of-stake systems, are not quantum-resistant and require transitioning to quantum-secure alternatives. This paper focuses on the spatial complexity implications of adopting quantum-secure signature schemes, specifically their impact on transaction and block sizes. Using Ethereum as a case study, this preliminary study highlights the challenges associated with increased data overhead. Future work will extend this analysis to include other performance dimensions, such as computational efficiency and network throughput, while also evaluating the security levels of quantum-resistant alternatives. Β© 2025 IEEE.
