AB  - In recent years, blockchain technology has introduced groundbreaking solutions, primarily due to its application in decentralized data storage and trustless transactions. At the heart of these systems are digital signatures, with elliptic curve cryptography being widely used. However, the advent of quantum computing poses a significant threat to the security of these cryptographic schemes. To address scalability and cost concerns, off-chain solutions like payment channel networks (PCNs) have gained attention. A recent example is the notion of adaptor signatures (AS), which plays a crucial role in enabling secure and efficient off-chain interactions, facilitating trustless atomic swaps and conditional payments. However, existing AS schemes in post-quantum settings often suffer from high communication overheads and fail to guarantee essential properties such as unlinkability and full extractability. To address this gap, we provide a more general and flexible approach to constructing adaptor signatures by leveraging cryptographic group actions. Our particular focus is on signature schemes based on free and transitive group actions where the acting group is abelian. As a versatile abstraction, group actions provide a unifying framework for constructing post-quantum secure primitives. Our generic construction of AS employs any signature scheme based on cryptographic group actions (SS-CGA) as a basic building block. More specifically, we define a variant of SS-CGA, called modified SS-CGA (MSS-CGA), which leverages a strong random self-reducible relation derived from group actions. Our proposed AS from MSS-CGA achieves the dual objectives of verifying the validity of a signature and securely releasing a witness upon signature verification. We rigorously prove the security of our scheme, demonstrating its resilience against pre-signature adaptability, strong-full extractability, unlinkability and witness hiding. As a concrete realization of our framework, we instantiate our generic construction using the isogeny-based signature scheme CSI-FiSh, resulting in the first post-quantum adaptor signature that seamlessly combines unlinkability and strong-full extractability. We further present a discrete-logarithm-based instantiation of our framework. Remarkably, our scheme achieves these properties with minimal overhead in communication cost and signature size compared to existing post-quantum alternatives. Β© 2026 American Institute of Mathematical Sciences. All rights reserved.
