AB  - The vehicle-to-grid (V2G) technology addresses the impact of renewable energy fluctuations on the smart grid, improving its efficiency and stability. However, V2G still faces many security threats, especially the risk of privacy leakage during cross-domain authentication of electric vehicles. Although many V2G cross-domain authentication schemes have been proposed, most of them are based on traditional cryptographic systems that cannot resist quantum attacks in the post-quantum era. To address this challenge, lattice-based cryptography, as a quantum-resistant cryptographic system, is used in V2G cross-domain authentication to resist quantum attacks. However, since lattice-based cryptography is computationally expensive and cannot meet the high real-time requirements of V2G cross-domain authentication, we introduce online/offline signatures and aggregation signatures and simplify the re-authentication process to improve the authentication efficiency. Therefore, we propose a lattice-based online/offline cross-domain authentication scheme in V2G, which is the first time that lattice-based online/offline signature technology is applied to the cross-domain authentication in V2G. Meanwhile, we introduce blockchain and smart contracts to store vehicle information. Furthermore, we use the random oracle model to prove the security of the scheme. Finally, performance evaluation shows that our scheme has low overheads and meets the real-time requirements of V2G. Β© 2004-2012 IEEE.
