AB  - Currently, the fifth-generation (5G) mobile communication system has been implemented in numerous countries and is being utilized extensively by consumers. The demand for 5G spectrum is high because of AI wearable applications like virtual reality (VR) headsets and gaming. The development of sixth-generation (6G) mobile communication systems marks a significant advancement in wireless technology, with the potential to bring about unprecedented improvements in connectivity, speed, and intelligence. The need for 6G arises from the substantial increase in mobile data traffic and the limitations of current 5G networks in meeting emerging demands, including holographic communication and digital twins. Unlike its predecessors, 6G is expected to offer ultra-high-speed data rates, ultra-low latency, and extensive coverage, enabling advanced applications in fields such as healthcare, autonomous systems, smart cities, and industrial automation. A major driving force behind the development of 6G is the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) into network operations. These technologies are anticipated to enable intelligent management and optimization of network resources, significantly enhancing the operational efficiency. The impact of AI extends beyond operational benefits, playing a crucial role in strengthening security mechanisms to combat advanced cyber threats, improving physical layer coding accuracy, and enabling real-time adaptive security measures. The deployment of AI-driven security solutions is essential to address the vulnerabilities associated with the increasing reliance on interconnected devices and networks. Terahertz (THz) frequencies form another fundamental aspect of the 6G technology, offering a substantial increase in bandwidth compared to existing spectrum allocations. However, this advancement comes with its own set of challenges, including increased susceptibility to signal attenuation and the need for advanced materials and antenna technologies to ensure effective signal propagation and reception. The integration of quantum technologies aims to enhance the security framework of 6G by providing encryption mechanisms strong enough to withstand the computational power of quantum computers. Together with blockchain and distributed ledger technologies, this approach aims to establish a secure and trustworthy communication environment. The evolution of network architectures in 6G includes the adoption of cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems, edge computing, and software-defined networking (SDN). These innovative architectures are designed to provide seamless connectivity, high reliability, and minimal latency, which are crucial for supporting essential applications and massive machine-type communications. Privacy preservation remains a critical concern for 6G networks. State-of-the-art privacy techniques, such as holographic encryption and differential privacy, are expected to safeguard user data while encouraging data-driven innovations. The challenge of maintaining privacy in an increasingly interconnected world requires ongoing advancements in privacy-preserving technologies and frameworks. The path toward 6G is characterized by significant technological advancements and complex challenges. The integration of AI, advanced security protocols, quantum technologies, and innovative network architectures will collectively shape the 6G landscape, advancing the vision of a hyper-connected, intelligent, and secure global communication network. With the essence of current research and development efforts aimed at realizing the full potential of 6G, paving the way for transformative applications and services will redefine the future of connectivity. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Sushil Kumar Singh, Rajendrasinh B. Jadeja, Ashish Khanna, Pushan Kumar Dutta, and Bal Virdee; individual chapters, the contributors.
