AB  - The manufacturing of railcars amalgamates multiple distributed manufacturing suppliers and facilities. Transportation manufacturing systems are dynamic, complex and large-scale hierarchical distributed environments, where digital integration and interoperability are a requisite. Convergence of emergent technologies i.e. cloud computing (CC), blockchain, artificial intelligence (AI) and Internet of Things (IoT) results in integration of multiple heterogeneous system-of-system (SoS) identified as cyber physical-production systems (CPPSs). CPPSs are a high value target for cyber-attacks, which demand system resiliency and security. Cybersecurity is fundamental to the success of CPPSs, when integrated with artificial intelligence of things (AIoT) systems. The study focused on the transition of the cybersecurity landscape subject to quantum reality for transportation manufacturing systems. A systems thinking methodology is utilized for SoS modelling for cloud-native applications system-wide protection built using reactive systems. The result is an architectural design for an integrated manufacturing supply chain system with advanced cybersecurity i.e. quantum-based cyber-physical production systems (QCPPS). A hybrid quantum computing (QC) and classical system composed of quantum approximate optimization algorithm (QAOA), utilizing multi-level split federated learning (SFL) is adopted for security and operations. Β© 2025 Computers and Industrial Engineering. All rights reserved.
