TI  - 6G-Enabled IoT for Fault-Tolerant Design in Consumer Electronics A Predictive Maintenance Approach
PY  - 2025
T2  - Security Paradigms in 6G Smart Cities and IoT Ecosystems: Navigating the Future
SP  - 432
EP  - 444
DO  - 10.1201/9781003564645-30
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105025307699?origin=resultslist
AD  - Faculty of Computer Science and Engineering, Poornima University, Rajasthan, Jaipur, India
AB  - The rapid expansion of connected consumer electronics and smart ecosystems has created significant challenges in data processing, security, scalability, and performance. Traditional computing systems struggle to efficiently manage the massive influx of data generated by smart homes, wearables, and autonomous devices. The integration of 6G and Internet of Things (IoT)-enabled fault-tolerant architectures, along with Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency Communication (URLLC) and quantum computing (QC), presents a transformative solution, enabling real-time predictive analytics, enhanced decision-making, and robust optimization applications for next-generation consumer electronics. IoT-driven intelligent monitoring systems, supported by URLLC, enhance security, anomaly detection, and data-driven optimizations, ensuring resilience in mission-critical applications by providing ultra-fast, high-reliability data exchange. Additionally, artificial intelligence (AI)-optimized edge computing in 6G networks, combined with URLLCβ€™s near-zero latency, enables smart consumer electronics to process and respond to device failures in real-time, ensuring energy-efficient, adaptive, and fault-tolerant performance. The incorporation of QC and quantum machine learning (QML) further revolutionizes predictive maintenance and optimization in consumer electronics. Quantum algorithms such as quantum approximate optimization algorithm (QAOA), and quantum key distribution (QKD) enhance fault detection, secure device communication, and optimize resource allocation in IoT-driven ecosystems. Quantum-enhanced AI models, leveraging Variational Quantum Circuits (VQC) and Quantum Support Vector Machines (QSVM), improve anomaly detection, fault tolerance, and decision-making efficiency, surpassing the limitations of classical computing in processing vast, real-time sensor data streams. A structured approach is adopted to integrate fault-tolerant design with 6G, IoT, and QC-based predictive analytics. First, industry-specific challenges related to real-time fault detection, optimization, and security are identified. Advanced AI-driven models, such as random forest, fuzzy Minβ€“Max (FMM), and deep learning techniques, are employed for anomaly detection in electromechanical components like actuators, motors, and gearboxes. Hybrid cloud-edge architectures powered by 6G ensure real-time fault reporting, leveraging secure blockchain frameworks for tamper-proof logging of device failures. As 6G technology matures, IoT networks expand, and QC hardware advances, the transition from reactive maintenance to AI- and QC-driven proactive fault prevention will redefine consumer electronics, making them smarter, more secure, and energy-efficient. Future advancements in digital twins, quantum-enhanced AI, AI-powered self-healing systems, and resilient IoT architectures will drive the next generation of intelligent consumer electronics, ensuring long-term sustainability, performance optimization, and real-time adaptability. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Sushil Kumar Singh, Rajendrasinh B. Jadeja, Ashish Khanna, Pushan Kumar Dutta, and Bal Virdee; individual chapters, the contributors.
