TI  - Biometric-Controlled, Quantum-Safe ABE with Blockchain for Secure Healthcare Data
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2nd International Conference on Intelligent Systems for Cybersecurity, ISCS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ISCS69371.2025.11386444
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034881102?origin=resultslist
AD  - Vellore Institute of Technology, School of Computer Science and Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Vellore, India
AB  - Quantum computing advancements are a significant threat to Electronic Health Record (EHR) security, posing a 'harvest now, decrypt later' threat that exposes patient data to being compromised for decades. Guaranteeing the integrity of this lifetime medical data is therefore essential because their unauthorized disclosure can expose patients to severe risks, from insurance discrimination to social stigma and professional reprisal. In addition, traditional access control frameworks fall short of offering actual data sovereignty and lack verifiable accountability mechanisms. In this paper, we introduce a new framework that couples quantum-safe cryptography with patient-driven control to address both concerns at hand. Our solution is based on a post-quantum Module-Learning with Errors (M-LWE) lattice-based Attribute-Based Encryption (ABE) scheme, hence, sensitive health data is securely and forever shielded. The framework has two fundamental contributions. First one is an access model controlled by biometrics where patients themselves manage and withdraw access to their information from user devices via secure off-chain authentication. This implies that no sophisticated key management systems are required and control is in the hands of the user. Second, a blockchain-audited ledger that securely stores every access event, such as grants and revocations, with an immutable trail for regulatory compliance and trust. In addition to this our architecture is also able to handle a decentralized multi-authority model for scalability across diverse healthcare institutions. The outcome is a holistic framework that ensures data confidentiality against future attack while establishing a new standard for auditable, patient-initiated health information exchange. Β© 2025 IEEE.
