AB  - Fast developments in digital technology have made strong security measures essential, as well as caused concerns about energy consumption. Emphasizing how sustainable computing may improve security measures without too high energy expenditures, this chapter analyzes the interaction between green computational intelligence (GCI) and cybersecurity. Combining distributed security architectures, energy-efficient machine learning algorithms, and quantum-safe encryption, GCI offers a paradigm that strikes environmental sustainability with cybersecurity robustness. We review emerging technologies like low-power device lightweight encryption, federated learning for safe IoT networks, and low-energy footprint blockchainβ€“based security mechanisms. Real-world applications in industrial automation, smart grids, and healthcare expose the pragmatic nature of these concepts. Furthermore, discussed in the chapter are problems such as the growing attack surface of distributed systems and techniques to lower risks without compromising performance. Using GCI-driven cybersecurity technologies can significantly reduce energy costs while maintaining high security, so ensuring a safe and sustainable digital future, according to the results. Recommendations for future studies and cross-industry cooperation to maximize energy-conscious cybersecurity solutions wrap up this chapter. Β© 2025 Scrivener Publishing LLC.
