AB  - Integration of Internet of Things (IoT) devices with new sixth-generation (6G) networks offers unparalleled connectivity, speed, and automation capabilities. But this convergence also brings major security challenges that need to be addressed as a matter of urgency. The increased use of IoT devices, as they enter diverse sectors β€“ from healthcare to smart cities β€“ extends the methods in which cyber threats can infiltrate a system. IoT is intrinsically a decentralized and heterogeneous environment, which, compounded with ultra-low-latency requirements as well as massive device connectivity in 6G, could escalate the exposure for such threats that existing security notions wonβ€™t help. In this chapter, we elaborate on the inevitable security challenges of IoT/6G networks, ranging from data privacy, authentication, and network integrity to advanced distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) mitigation against complex attacks. It also examines new approaches and tricks, such as quantum-resistant cryptography, artificial intelligence (AI)-driven threat detection, or decentralized security models based on blockchain technology. This chapter aims to provide a state-of-the-art view of the security aspect within such an IoT-6G framework, by making an analysis on risks and proposing original approaches in order to help researchers, policymakers, and industry stakeholders. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Gaganpreet Kaur, A.V. Senthil Kumar, G. Prasanna Lakshmi, and Rakesh Nyayapati individual chapters, the contributors.
